    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly, we need to find a square area that can be perfectly covered by these rectangles.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of one 2-by-3 rectangle:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one rectangle} = 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the smallest square whose area is a multiple of 6. Let's denote the side length of this square by \\( s \\). The area of the square will be \\( s^2 \\). Since \\( s^2 \\) must be a multiple of 6, \\( s^2 \\) must include the factors of 2 and 3.\n\nThe smallest square number that is a multiple of 6 is \\( 6^2 = 36 \\). Therefore, the side length of the square is \\( \\sqrt{36} = 6 \\).\n\nNow, we need to determine how many 2-by-3 rectangles are required to cover this 6-by-6 square. To do this, we calculate the number of rectangles needed along the length and the width of the square.\n\n1. **Number of rectangles along the length:**\n   - The length of the square is 6 units.\n   - Each rectangle has a width of 2 units.\n   - The number of rectangles needed along the length is \\( \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\).\n\n2. **Number of rectangles along the width:**\n   - The width of the square is 6 units.\n   - Each rectangle has a height of 3 units.\n   - The number of rectangles needed along the width is \\( \\frac{6}{3} = 2 \\).\n\nTo find the total number of rectangles, we multiply the number of rectangles along the length by the number of rectangles along the width:\n\\[\n3 \\times 2 = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a 6-by-6 square region exactly is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
